      "content": "Challenge E — Practitioner credentialing\nScenario\nLarkhollow Community Hospital's Medical Staff Office credentials every practitioner who applies for membership and clinical privileges: it collects the application, verifies each element with the source that owns it, chases what has not come back, presents a complete file to the committees, and records what the Governing Body decides. One person does this work, by hand, for every file at once, and the office needs it run the same way every time — so that nothing is presented incomplete, nothing stale carries forward, and no privilege is ever active before the approval that grants it. The hospital, its practitioners and its records are fictional.\nYour task\nCreate your own repository from the starter, interview the office's Medical Services Professional, and build a reusable Agent Skill that runs an initial-appointment file from application intake to recorded appointment and active privileges.\nThe office releases its caseload as closed export batches. Your Skill reads one batch and the state your previous run left behind, and produces the next state and a human action queue for every file in it. Each batch carries its own export date; that date is the clock for everything computed from that batch, and it is never the date you run your Skill. Three batches are supplied, and you obtain them, and the office's own documents, through the stakeholder interview below. Run them in order, and make the third run resume from the second rather than start again.\nAcross those batches the Skill assembles each file, runs the office's verification checks, records what each element's state is and what is still owed on it, computes whether the packet is presentable and binds that statement to one application revision, consumes the committee and Governing Body decision records the office supplies, records the appointment and activates exactly what was approved from the day the decision permits. It also handles what