      "content": "Accepted sources by element — who may attest\nLarkhollow Community Hospital · Medical Staff Office Table version LARK-ATT-2026.1, in force from 2026-01-01. Maintained by the Medical Services Professional.\nThe manual we adopted leaves its source lists open. Section 6, Subsection 4, element 2 ends \"etc., as long as the source is a designated equivalent source for education and training verifications\", and element 5 ends \"or other nationally recognized certifying body, as long as the source is a designated equivalent source for board certification verifications\".\nLarkhollow rule. An open list cannot be applied the same way twice, so ours is closed. This table is the whole list. A reply from a source on it is admissible. A reply from any source not on it is inadmissible and is raised to the Medical Services Professional — it is not quietly accepted, and it is not quietly treated as a secondary source.\nTwo rules cut across every row, both the manual's, both from Section 6, Subsection 1:\n\"Primary source verifications received directly from the applicant cannot be used.\"\n\"Copies of a diploma, license, registration, etc., in place of verifications are unacceptable.\"\nSo the applicant is never an accepted source for any element, on any row below, however genuine the document they carry. The reply is filed as received and the request stays open.\nEducation and training\nClass\nAccepted sources\nPrimary source\nThe degree-granting school's registrar's office or dean's office. The sponsoring institution's graduate medical education office or programme director, for a residency or fellowship\nDesignated equivalent source\nEducational Commission for Foreign Medical Graduates (ECFMG). Federation Credentials Verification Service of the Federation of State Medical Boards (FCVS). American Medical Association Physician Profile. American Osteopathic Association Physician Profile. National Student Clearinghouse\nSecondary source — only on impossibility\nThe applicant's most recent affiliation's medical staff office, stating how it obtained its own verification, when the school or programme has closed and no designated equivalent source holds the record. The reason is written into the file\nNot accepted\nThe applicant. An alumni association. A departmental colleague writing in a personal capacity. A recruiter, staffing agency or locum tenens agency. A photocopied or scanned certificate from any hand\nLicensure\nClass\nAccepted sources\nPrimary source\nThe issuing state licensing board, through its own online verification service or a written reply from the board\nDesignated equivalent source\nFederation of State Medical Boards Physician Data Center\nSecondary source\nNone. A state licensing board does not close\nNot accepted\nThe applicant. A prior employer's copy of a licence. An aggregator or directory not named above\nBoard certification\nClass\nAccepted sources\nPrimary source\nThe issuing certifying board, through its own verification service or a written reply\nDesignated equivalent source\nAmerican Board of Medical Specialties (ABMS) certification verification. CertiFACTS. American Medical Association Physician Profile. American Osteopathic Association Physician Profile. American Board of Physician Specialties (ABPS). American Nurses Credentialing Center (ANCC). National Commission on Certification of Physician Assistants (NCCPA)\nSecondary source\nNone\nNot accepted\nThe applicant. A certificate image. A specialty society membership roster\nAffiliations and employment\nClass\nAccepted sources\nPrimary source\nThe affiliated hospital's medical staff office. The employer's human resources department or the practice's authorized administrator\nDesignated equivalent source\nA credentials verification organization retained by that affiliation or employer, where the reply names the organization and the affiliation it acted for\nSecondary source — only on impossibility\nWhere the employer has dissolved and no successor holds the records, a written account from the applicant's most recent surviving affiliation of what that affiliation verified, with the reason written into the file\nNot accepted\nThe applicant. A supervisor writing from a personal address without the employer's authority. A professional networking profile. A letter of recommendation offered in place of a dates-and-position verification\nWhat to do with a reply that is not on this table\nRecord it as received, with who sent it and how it arrived. Nothing is discarded.\nMark the entry still outstanding. An inadmissible reply does not resolve anything.\nKeep requesting from a source that is on the table, and keep logging each attempt.\nRaise it to the Medical Services Professional, who decides whether the case is one of the impossibility routes above.\nNon-response is not impossibility. A source that exists and does not answer is a non-responsive source: it stays outstanding, the attempts keep being logged, and the manual's red flag applies. Only a source that no longer exists opens the secondary route."
