      "content": "Privilege criteria — initial appointment\nLarkhollow Community Hospital · Medical Staff Office Criteria version LARK-PRIV-2026.1, in force from 2026-01-01. Set by the Clinical Director with the Executive Committee of the Medical Staff. Published and applied by the Medical Staff Office. The office applies these criteria; the office does not write them and does not extend them for an individual applicant.\nEvery decision record cites the criteria version it was decided under. A file decided under one version is not re-read under another; a new version re-opens the mapping for files not yet decided.\nThe manual we adopted says what criteria must cover, in Section 9: \"At a minimum, privilege criteria should address the required education level, formal training requirements, and current experience.\" The three columns below are those three.\nThe twelve privilege groups\nEvery group requires a Doctor of Medicine or Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine degree from an accredited school, so the education level is not repeated in each row.\nCode\nPrivilege group\nCertifications that support it\nMinimum training\nCurrent experience\nPRIV-FM\nFamily Medicine — outpatient and inpatient care of patients of any age\nAmerican Board of Family Medicine (ABFM), Family Medicine; American Osteopathic Board of Family Physicians (AOBFP), Family Practice\nCompletion of an accredited family medicine residency, 36 months\nPatient care in the specialty within the last 24 months\nPRIV-IM\nInternal Medicine — outpatient and inpatient care of adults\nAmerican Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM), Internal Medicine; American Osteopathic Board of Internal Medicine (AOBIM), Internal Medicine\nCompletion of an accredited internal medicine residency, 36 months\nPatient care in the specialty within the last 24 months\nPRIV-HOSP\nHospital Medicine — adult inpatient care, admission and attending\nAmerican Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM), Internal Medicine; American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM), Internal Medicine with Focused Practice in Hospital Medicine; American Board of Family Medicine (ABFM), Family Medicine; American Osteopathic Board of Internal Medicine (AOBIM), Internal Medicine\nCompletion of an accredited internal medicine residency or an accredited family medicine residency, 36 months\nInpatient care within the last 24 months\nPRIV-CARD\nCardiovascular Disease — consultative cardiology, non-invasive testing\nAmerican Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM), Cardiovascular Disease; American Osteopathic Board of Internal Medicine (AOBIM), Cardiology\nCompletion of an accredited cardiovascular disease fellowship, 36 months, in addition to an accredited internal medicine residency\nCardiology practice within the last 24 months\nPRIV-EM\nEmergency Medicine — emergency department care of patients of any age\nAmerican Board of Emergency Medicine (ABEM), Emergency Medicine; American Osteopathic Board of Emergency Medicine (AOBEM), Emergency Medicine\nCompletion of an accredited emergency medicine residency, 36 months\nEmergency department practice within the last 24 months\nPRIV-GS\nGeneral Surgery — elective and emergency general surgical procedures\nAmerican Board of Surgery (ABS), Surgery; American Osteopathic Board of Surgery (AOBS), General Surgery\nCompletion of an accredited general surgery residency, 60 months\nOperative practice within the last 24 months\nPRIV-OBG\nObstetrics and Gynecology — obstetric and gynaecological care and surgery\nAmerican Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology (ABOG), Obstetrics and Gynecology; American Osteopathic Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology (AOBOG)\nCompletion of an accredited obstetrics and gynecology residency, 48 months\nObstetric or gynaecological practice within the last 24 months\nPRIV-PED\nPediatrics — outpatient and inpatient care of patients under 18\nAmerican Board of Pediatrics (ABP), Pediatrics; American Osteopathic Board of Pediatrics (AOBP), Pediatrics\nCompletion of an accredited pediatrics residency, 36 months\nPatient care in the specialty within the last 24 months\nPRIV-ANES\nAnesthesiology — general and regional anaesthesia, peri-operative care\nAmerican Board of Anesthesiology (ABA), Anesthesiology; American Osteopathic Board of Anesthesiology (AOBA)\nCompletion of an accredited anesthesiology residency, 48 months\nAnaesthetic practice within the last 24 months\nPRIV-RAD\nDiagnostic Radiology — interpretation of diagnostic imaging\nAmerican Board of Radiology (ABR), Diagnostic Radiology; American Osteopathic Board of Radiology (AOBR), Diagnostic Radiology\nCompletion of an accredited diagnostic radiology residency, 48 months\nInterpretive practice within the last 24 months\nPRIV-PSY\nPsychiatry — inpatient and outpatient psychiatric care of adults\nAmerican Board of Psychiatry and Neurology (ABPN), Psychiatry; American Osteopathic Board of Neurology and Psychiatry (AOBNP), Psychiatry\nCompletion of an accredited psychiatry residency, 48 months\nPsychiatric practice within the last 24 months\nPRIV-ORTHO\nOrthopaedic Surgery — elective and trauma orthopaedic procedures\nAmerican Board of Orthopaedic Surgery (ABOS), Orthopaedic Surgery; American Osteopathic Board of Orthopedic Surgery (AOBOS), Orthopedic Surgery\nCompletion of an accredited orthopaedic surgery residency, 60 months\nOperative practice within the last 24 months\nThe boards named above are real certifying bodies. Nothing in these materials is a statement about any practitioner they have actually certified.\nHow the office applies the table\nA requested group is supported when the applicant holds a verified certification from one of the boards named in that group's row, in the specialty named, with certification_status Active on the decision date, and the verified training meets the minimum training requirement.\nA requested group is unsupported when neither condition holds. The finding names that group. Other requested groups are unaffected.\nAn inactive or expired certification does not support a privilege group. It is still verified and still recorded — what the applicant is certified in and what the applicant was once certified in are different facts — but it supports nothing.\nBoard eligible is accepted in place of an active certification for PRIV-FM, PRIV-IM, PRIV-HOSP and PRIV-PED only, and only when the issuing board confirms in writing that the applicant is a candidate in good standing and states the date the eligibility ends, and that date is in the future on the decision date. Board eligible is not accepted for PRIV-CARD, PRIV-EM, PRIV-GS, PRIV-OBG, PRIV-ANES, PRIV-RAD, PRIV-PSY or PRIV-ORTHO.\nPRIV-CARD requires both: the cardiovascular disease fellowship and the internal medicine residency that precedes it. A cardiology certification without the residency, or the residency without the fellowship, does not support it.\nWhen nothing the applicant holds supports any requested group, this is a threshold eligibility question. The office raises it to the Clinical Director, who decides whether the application is discontinued. The office does not discontinue an application. The office raises it on what has been verified at the export date: while nothing yet verified supports any requested group, the question stands with the Clinical Director, and it is withdrawn without a disposition when a later verification supports the request.\nThe current-experience column is not the office's to apply. Current competency is determined by clinical leadership, not the office (the manual, Section 6, Subsection 4, element 8), and it is one of the elements this procedure does not handle. Whether a group is supported is decided by rules 1 to 6 alone; the current-experience requirement travels with the packet for the committee and never makes a group unsupported.\nWhether a supported request should be granted is not settled here. That is the Executive Committee of the Medical Staff's recommendation and the Governing Body's decision."
